/T (  Aliasing, shallow and deep copy. alias = orig creates no copy, just another name for the same list. copy.copy\(orig\) and orig[:] create a new outer list, but the inner lists are still shared, so changing an inner listshowsinboth\(theoutputshows99intheshallowcopies\).copy.deepcopycopiestheinner lists too,sothedeepcopykept[1,2].Ashallowcopyisenoughwhentheelementsareimmutable \(numbers, strings, tuples\) or only the outer list will change. A deep copy is needed when nested mutable objects will be changed independently.)
/E (  Aliasing, shallow and deep copy. alias = orig creates no copy, just another name for the same list. copy.copy\(orig\) and orig[:] create a new outer list, but the inner lists are still shared, so changing an inner listshowsinboth\(theoutputshows99intheshallowcopies\).copy.deepcopycopiestheinner lists too,sothedeepcopykept[1,2].Ashallowcopyisenoughwhentheelementsareimmutable \(numbers, strings, tuples\) or only the outer list will change. A deep copy is needed when nested mutable objects will be changed independently.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [27]
>>
