647 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 204 0 R
/T (Alarm minutes. enumerate\(\) supplies the minute number with each reading: [m for m, c in enumerate\(readings\) if valid and not 2 <= c <= 8]. Invalid sensor readings are not counted as alarms. List comprehension versus generator expression. A list comprehension builds the whole list in memory at once. A generator expression \(round brackets\) produces one value at a time when asked, stores nothing, and can be read only once. The measurements show 6,675,480 bytes for the list and 208 bytes for the generator object. )
/E (Alarm minutes. enumerate\(\) supplies the minute number with each reading: [m for m, c in enumerate\(readings\) if valid and not 2 <= c <= 8]. Invalid sensor readings are not counted as alarms. List comprehension versus generator expression. A list comprehension builds the whole list in memory at once. A generator expression \(round brackets\) produces one value at a time when asked, stores nothing, and can be read only once. The measurements show 6,675,480 bytes for the list and 208 bytes for the generator object. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
648 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 204 0 R
/T (Is a list comprehension always best? No. At 1,000,000 readings it was the fastest of the three versions \(about 25% faster than the loop\), and map\(\)/filter\(\) with lambda were close to the loop and harder to read. But when the result is only consumed once, as in sum\(...\), the generator takes almost the same time and needs O\(1\) memory instead of O\(n\). A loop is better when the logic is complex. Alternative concept. sum\(c for c in readings if -50 <= c <= 50\) never builds the list. Time is O\(n\) for both; memory is O\(n\) for the list and O\(1\) for the generator. )
/E (Is a list comprehension always best? No. At 1,000,000 readings it was the fastest of the three versions \(about 25% faster than the loop\), and map\(\)/filter\(\) with lambda were close to the loop and harder to read. But when the result is only consumed once, as in sum\(...\), the generator takes almost the same time and needs O\(1\) memory instead of O\(n\). A loop is better when the logic is complex. Alternative concept. sum\(c for c in readings if -50 <= c <= 50\) never builds the list. Time is O\(n\) for both; memory is O\(n\) for the list and O\(1\) for the generator. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [9]
>>
