2001 0 obj
<<
/Title (General form. [expression for item in iterable if condition]. Here: [c for c in readings if -50 <= c <= 50]. Comprehension or loop. A comprehension becomes harder to read than a loop when it has several nested for clauses, several conditions, or needs more than one statement \(for example logging a rejected reading\). The loop version is then clearer.)
/Dest [34 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1995 0 R
/Next 2007 0 R
>>
endobj
2002 0 obj
<<
/Title (Is a list comprehension always best? No. At 1,000,000 readings it was the fastest of the three versions \(about 25% faster than the loop\), and map\(\)/filter\(\) with lambda were close to the loop and harder to read. But when the result is only consumed once, as in sum\(...\), the generator takes almost the same time and needs O\(1\) memory instead of O\(n\). A loop is better when the logic is complex. Alternative concept. sum\(c for c in readings if -50 <= c <= 50\) never builds the list. Time is O\(n\) for both; memory is O\(n\) for the list and O\(1\) for the generator.)
/Dest [34 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1995 0 R
/Prev 2008 0 R
>>
endobj
